xnu 0335: the userdata partition as a read-only USB disk

The gadget's configuration grows a third interface: bulk-only mass storage
(SCSI transparent, class 8 subclass 6 protocol 0x50) on the endpoint pair the
ECM function leaves free, 0x02 OUT and 0x83 IN. The ECM function, the console
stream and the udp/5141 control channel are untouched, so a boot keeps its
remote control while the host mounts the iOS root read-only and can copy it,
fsck it, or diff it against a backup.

The medium is the GPT partition the root was mounted from, not a guess: the
UFS driver already parses `userdata` out of the entry table, and 0335 exports
one 512-byte-sector read of that range (mondrian_ufs_userdata_read), under the
same lock as the block device. The gadget answers INQUIRY (36 bytes: vendor
"MONDRIAN", product "iOS userdata", revision "1.0"), TEST UNIT READY, REQUEST
SENSE, MODE SENSE, READ CAPACITY(10), REPORT LUNS and READ(10); writes are
consumed and refused with DATA PROTECT, so the host sees a write-protected
disk.

Two things decide whether the function can work, and they are the two that
made the earlier attempt hang on the host's first INQUIRY: the storage IN
endpoint needs a TX FIFO of its own (FIFO 3 by mainline's numbering, not the
ECM's FIFO 1, and GTXFIFOSIZ(3) is usually empty after the bootloader sized
the FIFOs for fastboot), and the interface must not be advertised at all when
its endpoints cannot be brought up. The device descriptor moves to class 0 so
the host binds each interface by its own class, as a composite device must.

GET_MAX_LUN answers 0 instead of stalling, the bulk-only reset and a
CLEAR_FEATURE on either endpoint drop the command in flight, and every path
through the state machine ends in a CSW, which is what the host is waiting for
when it looks stuck.

